          "diff_hunk": "void read_nonNotFoundStorageExceptionDuringRead_rethrowsStorageException() throws Exception {\n+    GcsItemInfo itemInfo = GcsItemInfo.builder().setItemId(itemId).setSize(KB).build();\n+    ApiFuture<DisposableByteString> failedFuture = mock(ApiFuture.class);\n+    when(failedFuture.get(anyLong(), any()))\n+        .thenThrow(new ExecutionException(new StorageException(500, \"Internal Server Error\")));",
          "body": "![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)\n\nInstead of mocking `ApiFuture` and stubbing its `get` method (which involves raw types and unchecked warnings), you can use `ApiFutures.immediateFailedFuture` to create a completed future that naturally throws an `ExecutionException` wrapping the target exception when `get()` is called. This is more idiomatic and avoids unnecessary mocking.\n\n```java\n    ApiFuture<DisposableByteString> failedFuture =\n        ApiFutures.immediateFailedFuture(new StorageException(500, \"Internal Server Error\"));\n```",
